**Manuscript text (plate-normalized IC50 results, Holm-corrected):**

After plate normalization, neither evolved group had a significantly altered IC~50~ relative to the ancestor for any antifungal drug, and the two groups did not differ significantly from each other (Figure 2G). For fluconazole, both groups trended toward elevated tolerance — 1.60-fold in the 35°C-evolved group (one-sample *t*-test on plate-normalized log[IC~50~/ancestor IC~50~], *n* = 18 populations, Holm-corrected *p* = 0.128, 95% CI for fold change: [1.08, 2.38]) and 1.47-fold in the 40°C-evolved group (Holm-corrected *p* = 0.263, 95% CI: [1.00, 2.18]) — but neither survived correction for multiple comparisons. The two evolved groups did not differ from each other in fluconazole tolerance (40°C/35°C: 0.92-fold; Welch *t*-test, *n* = 18 per group, Holm-corrected *p* = 0.891, 95% CI: [0.54, 1.56]). For caspofungin, the 40°C-evolved group showed a marginal trend toward elevated tolerance relative to the ancestor (1.20-fold; Holm-corrected *p* = 0.319, 95% CI: [0.98, 1.49]), while the 35°C-evolved group was indistinguishable from the ancestor (1.08-fold; Holm-corrected *p* = 0.654); the two groups did not differ from each other (40°C/35°C: 1.11-fold; Holm-corrected *p* = 0.891, 95% CI: [0.84, 1.46]). Neither group showed a significant change in amphotericin B tolerance relative to the ancestor (35°C-evolved: 1.08-fold, Holm-corrected *p* = 0.654; 40°C-evolved: 0.82-fold, Holm-corrected *p* = 0.604) or relative to each other (40°C/35°C: 0.76-fold; Holm-corrected *p* = 0.337, 95% CI: [0.53, 1.07]).

