**Manuscript text (PCA results):**

The first two PCs together explained 60.3% of within-group variance. PC1 (31.3%) was characterized by co-loading of T~h~ (loading = −0.570), fluconazole tolerance (−0.544), and amphotericin B tolerance (−0.497) in the same direction, with caspofungin contributing negligibly (loading = 0.078). PC2 (29.0%) captured an independent axis of TPC shape variation, with T~opt~ (0.667) and CT~max~ (−0.679) loading in opposing directions and drug tolerance variables near zero. The co-loading of T~h~ with fluconazole and amphotericin B tolerance on PC1 reveals a shared axis of within-group variation spanning thermal deactivation and drug tolerance. Notably, fluconazole and amphotericin B both target ergosterol — fluconazole by inhibiting its synthesis and amphotericin B by binding it directly — while caspofungin acts on a mechanistically distinct target, cell wall β-glucan synthesis. The drug-specific pattern of co-variation with T~h~ therefore maps onto this mechanistic distinction, suggesting that the cross-tolerance signal on PC1 may reflect a shared dependence on membrane ergosterol composition across thermal adaptation and membrane-active antifungal resistance.